# The artifact cache must INVALIDATE when a transitively-imported module's
# bytes change (issues/yo-build-artifact-cache-serves-a-stale-binary.md).
#
# The two existing cache cases cover the other two directions — `build-cache`
# asserts an unchanged tree HITS, and `build-depfile-scoped-stamp` asserts an
# unrelated new file does not over-invalidate. Nothing asserted that a CHANGED
# input invalidates at all, which is the direction this case adds.
#
# Scope, stated honestly: an edit landing BETWEEN two builds invalidated
# correctly even before the stale-binary fix, so this case does not by itself
# gate that fix — the defect there needed an edit landing DURING a compile, and
# the harness runs argv, not a shell, so it cannot schedule one. What does gate
# the fix is `build-cache`: if the depfile ever loses its per-input hashes, the
# record pass refuses to write a stamp it cannot trust, no build ever caches,
# and that case fails.
#
# The harness runs argv, not a shell, so a case cannot edit a source file
# directly. `yo add ./mylib` is the mutation: it rewrites `yo.toml`, which the
# artifact stamp covers because the manifest decides what `import("dep")`
# resolves to. The second build must therefore recompile.
#
# The kept substrings are the two mutually exclusive outcomes, so the golden
# records WHICH happened rather than merely that something did: `to compile`
# (the chunk scheduler ran) vs `cached: inputs unchanged, skipping compile`.
# A regression flips the second build from the first to the second.
stdout_keep_match=(cached: inputs unchanged, skipping compile|to compile)
timeout=240
